1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,280
When a subsea well heats up, the conductor and the casings want to grow.

2
00:00:04,380 --> 00:00:06,866
If you hold the wellhead so it cannot move,

3
00:00:06,966 --> 00:00:09,633
something has to carry that blocked expansion.

4
00:00:09,733 --> 00:00:11,497
That force is the lock-in load.

5
00:00:12,017 --> 00:00:15,107
In this video we calculate it for two conductor sizes.

6
00:00:15,207 --> 00:00:17,175
A thirty inch with a one inch wall,

7
00:00:17,275 --> 00:00:20,365
and a thirty six inch with a one and a half inch wall.

8
00:00:20,465 --> 00:00:25,091
After one year of production, holding them down takes about nineteen and a half,

9
00:00:25,191 --> 00:00:26,745
and twenty four meganewtons.

10
00:00:26,845 --> 00:00:30,113
Roughly two thousand, and two and a half thousand tonnes.

11
00:00:30,650 --> 00:00:35,278
We will build that number step by step by hand, check it against the full model,

12
00:00:35,378 --> 00:00:37,878
look at what the soil does, and does not do,

13
00:00:37,978 --> 00:00:40,951
and finally size the piles it takes to hold it down.

14
00:00:42,283 --> 00:00:45,414
Start with the forces on the wellhead once the well is hot.

15
00:00:45,514 --> 00:00:49,575
The inner casings and the tubing heat up, and push up through their hangers.

16
00:00:50,117 --> 00:00:54,696
The conductor, the twenty inch surface casing and the cement between them are

17
00:00:54,796 --> 00:00:56,094
bonded into one column.

18
00:00:56,194 --> 00:01:00,469
Near the seabed it heats up too, and pushes up on the wellhead housings.

19
00:01:01,017 --> 00:01:03,176
If the wellhead is not allowed to move,

20
00:01:03,276 --> 00:01:06,247
the restraint must push down with the sum of the two.

21
00:01:06,347 --> 00:01:07,695
That is the lock-in load.

22
00:01:08,217 --> 00:01:10,961
And the load path does not end at the restraint.

23
00:01:11,061 --> 00:01:15,702
The restraint has to be anchored, so the same force leaves through its anchor as

24
00:01:15,802 --> 00:01:16,117
uplift.

25
00:01:16,217 --> 00:01:20,680
Below the seabed, the column's compression is passed to the soil by friction,

26
00:01:20,780 --> 00:01:24,295
and at the conductor shoe, into the twenty inch and the rock.

27
00:01:25,617 --> 00:01:27,293
Both cases use the same well.

28
00:01:27,393 --> 00:01:31,395
A vertical oil producer in three hundred and fifty metres of water,

29
00:01:31,495 --> 00:01:34,886
with a twenty inch surface casing cemented to the seabed,

30
00:01:34,986 --> 00:01:36,601
and the usual inner strings.

31
00:01:37,750 --> 00:01:42,117
Option A is a thirty inch by one inch conductor in a thirty six inch hole,

32
00:01:42,217 --> 00:01:45,921
with five hundred and eighty eight square centimetres of steel.

33
00:01:46,021 --> 00:01:50,871
Option B is a thirty six inch by one and a half inch conductor in a forty two inch

34
00:01:50,971 --> 00:01:53,225
hole, with one thousand and forty nine.

35
00:01:53,325 --> 00:01:54,614
Almost twice the steel.

36
00:01:55,150 --> 00:01:59,030
The oil reaches the wellhead at about one hundred and eight degrees,

37
00:01:59,130 --> 00:02:01,780
a hundred and two above the seabed temperature.

38
00:02:01,880 --> 00:02:06,228
Just below the seabed, the thirty inch conductor warms by sixty one degrees,

39
00:02:06,328 --> 00:02:09,856
the thirty six inch by fifty five because it sits further out,

40
00:02:09,956 --> 00:02:11,963
and the twenty inch by seventy five.

41
00:02:12,063 --> 00:02:15,709
Without a heat transfer model, these are roughly zero point six,

42
00:02:15,809 --> 00:02:20,274
zero point five four, and zero point seven four of the fluid temperature rise.

43
00:02:21,617 --> 00:02:23,297
Now the hand calculation.

44
00:02:23,397 --> 00:02:28,925
For each bonded component, the force needed to stop it expanding is E, times A,

45
00:02:29,025 --> 00:02:30,848
times alpha, times delta T.

46
00:02:31,383 --> 00:02:36,437
For the thirty inch conductor, the steel area is zero point zero five eight eight

47
00:02:36,537 --> 00:02:37,328
square metres.

48
00:02:37,428 --> 00:02:39,873
Times two hundred and seven gigapascals,

49
00:02:39,973 --> 00:02:42,800
times twelve point four millionths per degree,

50
00:02:42,900 --> 00:02:46,427
that is one hundred and fifty one kilonewtons per degree.

51
00:02:46,527 --> 00:02:49,735
Times sixty one degrees, nine point two meganewtons.

52
00:02:50,283 --> 00:02:54,661
Do the same for the twenty inch and for both cement sheaths, and add them up.

53
00:02:54,761 --> 00:02:58,034
Sixteen point four meganewtons for the thirty inch system,

54
00:02:58,134 --> 00:03:00,999
and twenty three point two for the thirty six inch.

55
00:03:01,517 --> 00:03:04,317
Steel carries about eighty five percent of it.

56
00:03:04,417 --> 00:03:07,595
And the total depends on steel area and temperature,

57
00:03:07,695 --> 00:03:09,675
not on how long the conductor is.

58
00:03:09,775 --> 00:03:13,773
A longer pipe wants to grow more, but it is also a softer spring,

59
00:03:13,873 --> 00:03:15,412
so the length cancels out.

60
00:03:16,749 --> 00:03:18,848
The inner strings add their own push.

61
00:03:18,948 --> 00:03:22,472
Here, use the average temperature rise over each free length,

62
00:03:22,572 --> 00:03:26,690
from the hanger down to the top of cement, because it falls with depth.

63
00:03:27,216 --> 00:03:31,140
The thirteen and three eighths gives two point four meganewtons,

64
00:03:31,240 --> 00:03:35,666
the nine and five eighths one point six, and the tubing zero point five.

65
00:03:35,766 --> 00:03:40,759
Four and a half in total, or four point three after the small relief from helical

66
00:03:40,859 --> 00:03:41,325
buckling.

67
00:03:41,425 --> 00:03:43,525
It is the same for both conductors.

68
00:03:44,049 --> 00:03:48,407
Add it to the conductor system, and the hand estimate is twenty point nine

69
00:03:48,507 --> 00:03:53,225
meganewtons for the thirty inch, and twenty seven point seven for the thirty six

70
00:03:53,325 --> 00:03:53,526
inch.

71
00:03:53,626 --> 00:03:57,923
These are upper bounds, because every component is assumed fully blocked.

72
00:03:59,249 --> 00:04:01,007
So, does the soil hold it down?

73
00:04:01,107 --> 00:04:02,326
The model includes it.

74
00:04:02,426 --> 00:04:07,121
Every metre of the column sits on a soil spring that gives skin friction against

75
00:04:07,221 --> 00:04:07,960
the hole wall.

76
00:04:08,483 --> 00:04:12,115
But friction only acts where the pipe slides past the soil.

77
00:04:12,215 --> 00:04:15,658
A restrained column is held at the top by the restraint,

78
00:04:15,758 --> 00:04:18,947
and below about a hundred metres by cement and rock.

79
00:04:19,047 --> 00:04:22,110
In between, it moves only two to five millimetres.

80
00:04:22,210 --> 00:04:25,337
So the soil cannot take the load off the restraint.

81
00:04:25,883 --> 00:04:30,632
The little relief there is comes from the column pushing down into the soil below

82
00:04:30,732 --> 00:04:31,470
the restraint.

83
00:04:31,570 --> 00:04:34,643
Soft soil lets it do more of that, so the load drops.

84
00:04:34,743 --> 00:04:39,133
Stiff soil stops it, so the load climbs towards the hand calculation bound.

85
00:04:39,683 --> 00:04:40,753
Here is the effect.

86
00:04:40,853 --> 00:04:45,374
Make the soil ten times weaker, and the thirty inch lock-in load falls from

87
00:04:45,474 --> 00:04:48,085
nineteen and a half to fourteen meganewtons.

88
00:04:48,185 --> 00:04:51,967
Make it four times stronger, and it rises to twenty and a half.

89
00:04:52,067 --> 00:04:54,370
For a free wellhead it is the opposite.

90
00:04:54,470 --> 00:04:59,052
Weak soil lets it grow two hundred millimetres, stiff soil only thirty five.

91
00:04:59,583 --> 00:05:02,055
Where the soil really matters is elsewhere.

92
00:05:02,155 --> 00:05:04,507
Its friction capacity over the conductor,

93
00:05:04,607 --> 00:05:09,351
about twelve and fourteen and a half meganewtons, is less than the blocked force.

94
00:05:09,451 --> 00:05:13,658
So a free conductor slides, and grows about forty millimetres by itself.

95
00:05:13,758 --> 00:05:18,144
And the restraint's own anchor has to hold the full lock-in load in uplift,

96
00:05:18,244 --> 00:05:19,160
in the same soil.

97
00:05:20,483 --> 00:05:24,694
The full model gives nineteen and a half meganewtons for the thirty inch,

98
00:05:24,794 --> 00:05:27,648
and twenty four point one for the thirty six inch.

99
00:05:27,748 --> 00:05:30,542
Seven and thirteen percent below the hand bounds.

100
00:05:30,642 --> 00:05:35,090
The conductor steel is the biggest share, and the only one that changes much.

101
00:05:35,190 --> 00:05:38,339
Eight and a half, against twelve point six meganewtons.

102
00:05:38,883 --> 00:05:41,580
A restraint that gives a little relieves a lot.

103
00:05:41,680 --> 00:05:45,984
Halving the growth still costs about eight and a half to nine meganewtons,

104
00:05:46,084 --> 00:05:50,388
and every millimetre held back costs a third, to almost half a meganewton.

105
00:05:50,916 --> 00:05:53,889
And the load keeps creeping up as the rock warms.

106
00:05:53,989 --> 00:05:57,087
Twelve to fourteen meganewtons after the first day,

107
00:05:57,187 --> 00:05:59,846
twenty one and twenty seven after ten years.

108
00:05:59,946 --> 00:06:02,291
So design for end of life temperatures.

109
00:06:02,391 --> 00:06:06,556
There, the hand bounds are twenty three, and thirty one meganewtons.

110
00:06:07,883 --> 00:06:10,071
A few more effects change the picture.

111
00:06:10,171 --> 00:06:14,167
The lock-in load comes on top of the loads already in the conductor,

112
00:06:14,267 --> 00:06:19,106
from the wellhead and tree, the hanging casings, and riser loads during workovers.

113
00:06:19,206 --> 00:06:23,985
And every shut in and start up cycles it, so it is a fatigue load, not a one off.

114
00:06:24,516 --> 00:06:26,547
It scales roughly with temperature.

115
00:06:26,647 --> 00:06:31,418
About zero point two meganewtons for every degree of fluid temperature rise with

116
00:06:31,518 --> 00:06:35,437
the thirty inch, and zero point two four with the thirty six inch.

117
00:06:35,983 --> 00:06:38,440
And the highest stress is not at the seabed.

118
00:06:38,540 --> 00:06:42,973
At the conductor shoe, the conductor hands its compression to the twenty inch.

119
00:06:43,073 --> 00:06:47,565
There the twenty inch locally reaches about three hundred and eight megapascals

120
00:06:47,665 --> 00:06:52,389
with the thirty inch conductor, and three hundred and fifty two with the thirty six

121
00:06:52,489 --> 00:06:52,680
inch.

122
00:06:52,780 --> 00:06:56,109
Eighty one, and ninety three percent of K fifty five yield.

123
00:06:57,449 --> 00:06:59,213
So how big must the anchor be?

124
00:06:59,313 --> 00:07:02,504
Take a restraint frame held down by four steel piles.

125
00:07:02,604 --> 00:07:06,231
The frame pushes down on the wellhead with the lock-in load,

126
00:07:06,331 --> 00:07:11,385
and each pile carries a quarter of it, as uplift, for as long as the well produces.

127
00:07:11,916 --> 00:07:16,798
Size them for the end of life hand bounds, twenty three and thirty one meganewtons.

128
00:07:16,898 --> 00:07:21,360
That is five point seven meganewtons per pile for the thirty inch conductor,

129
00:07:21,460 --> 00:07:24,122
and seven point seven for the thirty six inch.

130
00:07:24,222 --> 00:07:28,924
About five hundred and eighty, and seven hundred and ninety tonnes on each pile.

131
00:07:29,024 --> 00:07:33,606
A sustained, cycling pull in clay deserves a safety factor of two on the soil,

132
00:07:33,706 --> 00:07:36,427
so each pile needs roughly twice that capacity.

133
00:07:36,949 --> 00:07:39,165
In uplift, a pile has no end bearing.

134
00:07:39,265 --> 00:07:41,918
It holds with skin friction along its shaft.

135
00:07:42,018 --> 00:07:46,111
Alpha, times the undrained shear strength, times the circumference,

136
00:07:46,211 --> 00:07:49,615
added up over the length, plus its own submerged weight.

137
00:07:49,715 --> 00:07:54,559
Our clay gets stronger with depth, so the friction grows with the square of the

138
00:07:54,659 --> 00:07:54,997
length.

139
00:07:55,516 --> 00:08:00,320
Solve for the length, and here are the piles, drawn to scale next to the conductor.

140
00:08:00,420 --> 00:08:05,224
For the thirty inch conductor, four piles one and a half metres wide and fifty five

141
00:08:05,324 --> 00:08:08,651
metres long, or two metres wide and forty six metres long.

142
00:08:08,751 --> 00:08:12,019
For the thirty six inch, sixty six, or fifty five metres.

143
00:08:12,119 --> 00:08:15,269
Each pile weighs eighty to a hundred and thirty tonnes.

144
00:08:15,816 --> 00:08:19,662
Length beats diameter, because the deep clay is the strongest.

145
00:08:19,762 --> 00:08:24,563
Doubling the diameter from one to two metres saves only twenty five to thirty

146
00:08:24,663 --> 00:08:26,728
metres, and the pile gets heavier.

147
00:08:26,828 --> 00:08:31,119
The steel itself is barely loaded, about thirty to forty megapascals,

148
00:08:31,219 --> 00:08:35,575
so the wall thickness is set by driving and handling, not by the pull.

149
00:08:36,116 --> 00:08:38,208
The length is sensitive to the soil.

150
00:08:38,308 --> 00:08:41,130
If cycling wears the friction down by a quarter,

151
00:08:41,230 --> 00:08:43,931
the piles need about eight to ten metres more.

152
00:08:44,031 --> 00:08:47,645
With a safety factor of one and a half, or full API friction,

153
00:08:47,745 --> 00:08:50,262
they get about eight to ten metres shorter.

154
00:08:51,583 --> 00:08:53,570
But a pile is not a rigid anchor.

155
00:08:53,670 --> 00:08:56,100
To carry load, the steel has to stretch,

156
00:08:56,200 --> 00:08:59,516
and the soil friction needs a little slip to build up.

157
00:08:59,616 --> 00:09:04,134
At its design load, a one and a half metre pile rises about ten to twelve

158
00:09:04,234 --> 00:09:04,893
millimetres.

159
00:09:05,416 --> 00:09:07,728
So the four piles act like a stiff spring,

160
00:09:07,828 --> 00:09:11,001
about two and a half to three meganewtons per millimetre.

161
00:09:11,101 --> 00:09:12,666
And the well is a spring too.

162
00:09:12,766 --> 00:09:17,087
The more the wellhead is allowed to rise, the less force it takes to hold it.

163
00:09:17,187 --> 00:09:19,728
Where the two lines cross, the system settles.

164
00:09:20,249 --> 00:09:24,701
With a rigid frame, the wellhead still rises seven to eight millimetres over ten

165
00:09:24,801 --> 00:09:28,058
years, instead of sixty one to sixty eight if it were free.

166
00:09:28,158 --> 00:09:32,780
And the restraint carries eighteen meganewtons instead of twenty one for the thirty

167
00:09:32,880 --> 00:09:36,706
inch, and twenty two instead of twenty seven for the thirty six inch.

168
00:09:36,806 --> 00:09:38,129
A real frame also flexes.

169
00:09:38,229 --> 00:09:42,282
At five meganewtons per millimetre, that gives ten to eleven millimetres,

170
00:09:42,382 --> 00:09:44,387
and seventeen and twenty meganewtons.

171
00:09:44,916 --> 00:09:48,239
So four piles hold back almost nine tenths of the growth,

172
00:09:48,339 --> 00:09:51,903
and carry about eighty five percent of the fully locked load.

173
00:09:52,003 --> 00:09:53,825
Bigger piles hardly change that.

174
00:09:53,925 --> 00:09:58,149
A slimmer, longer pile stretches more, and a fatter one needs more slip,

175
00:09:58,249 --> 00:10:00,431
so the stiffness stays about the same.

176
00:10:00,531 --> 00:10:04,335
Holding the wellhead truly still would take a far stiffer anchor.

177
00:10:04,435 --> 00:10:09,080
So size the piles for the full lock-in load, and take the small rise as margin.

178
00:10:09,616 --> 00:10:11,024
A few practical points.

179
00:10:11,124 --> 00:10:16,529
Space the piles at least three to five diameters apart, and away from the conductor.

180
00:10:16,629 --> 00:10:21,510
The frame carries twenty to thirty meganewtons in bending, so it is a heavy,

181
00:10:21,610 --> 00:10:23,149
template sized structure.

182
00:10:23,249 --> 00:10:26,950
Check creep and cyclic degradation under a sustained pull,

183
00:10:27,050 --> 00:10:29,507
and base it all on real site soil data.

184
00:10:29,607 --> 00:10:31,866
Suction anchors are the alternative,

185
00:10:31,966 --> 00:10:35,930
about six metres wide and nineteen to twenty four metres deep.

186
00:10:36,030 --> 00:10:41,042
But under a sustained pull the suction bleeds away, so count on friction only.

187
00:10:42,383 --> 00:10:45,778
So the design checks are the restraint frame and its piles,

188
00:10:45,878 --> 00:10:48,148
every component the load passes through,

189
00:10:48,248 --> 00:10:52,533
the conductor and the twenty inch at the seabed and at the conductor shoe,

190
00:10:52,633 --> 00:10:57,213
the thirteen and three eighths hanger lockdown, and fatigue for every start up.

191
00:10:57,749 --> 00:11:00,374
And to reduce the load: allow some movement,

192
00:11:00,474 --> 00:11:03,655
or engage the restraint only after the first heat up.

193
00:11:03,755 --> 00:11:06,441
Reduce the temperature with insulated tubing.

194
00:11:06,541 --> 00:11:09,599
Choose the conductor with the lock-in load in mind.

195
00:11:09,699 --> 00:11:13,066
And make sure the twenty inch is cemented to the seabed.

196
00:11:13,616 --> 00:11:14,070
In short.

197
00:11:14,170 --> 00:11:19,182
The lock-in load is the blocked thermal force of everything bonded at the wellhead,

198
00:11:19,282 --> 00:11:21,338
plus the push of the inner strings.

199
00:11:21,438 --> 00:11:23,617
Steel area times temperature sets it.

200
00:11:23,717 --> 00:11:25,526
The soil does not hold it down.

201
00:11:25,626 --> 00:11:28,359
It decides how far a free wellhead would grow,

202
00:11:28,459 --> 00:11:30,884
and how long the hold down piles must be.

203
00:11:30,984 --> 00:11:33,656
Here, four piles, one and a half metres wide,

204
00:11:33,756 --> 00:11:36,120
and fifty five to sixty six metres long.
